{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224279","model_call":26,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"reflective practitioner\" teachers (+ teaching instruction) (+ improve improvement) students)"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"reflective practitioner\" teachers (+ teaching instruction) (+ improve improvement) students)"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_169565317#3_343639226","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q560","rank":1,"snippet":"Practices and Assessment Skills 6) Connect Positively to the Whole-School Culture \" , \"segment\": \"however, they depend upon other great athletes and coaches to improve their performance over time. If they find themselves at a plateau in their performance, they look for mentors to push them beyond it. But in teaching, a promising young professional can fulfill that expectation without sufficient coaching for years. Of course, there is always teacher evaluation designed for that purpose; still, how often does it truly <cover>improve anything to do with the classroom? Even dedicated supervisors have precious little time to devote to the continuing development of teacher excellence. Teachers can be deceived by what is appealing, habitual and popular with students. Effective teaching is an acquired talent. Professional retreats offer teachers the opportunity to dedicate time to those qualitative steps that result in ongoing development. The six steps to becoming a master teacher include:\" , \"start_char\": 1623.000000 , \"title\": \"Six Steps to Master Teaching: Becoming a Reflective Practitioner | </cover>Edutopia\" , \"url\": \"https://www.edutopia.org/blog/master-teaching-margaret-regan\" }"},"time":84},"model_calls":26,"document_judgments":19,"relevance_3":14,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"model","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-224279","model_call":27,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","prompt_characters":56174,"model_calls":27,"document_judgments":19,"relevance_3":14,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"model","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224279","model_call":27,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input_tokens":12212,"output_tokens":258,"total_tokens":12470,"cached_tokens":11520,"reasoning_tokens":82},"output_characters":911,"decision":{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_39_169565317#3_343639226","relevance":2,"reason":"The Edutopia article on becoming a reflective practitioner supports the need for teachers to review their instruction by noting that effective teaching is acquired through ongoing development and that teachers can be misled by habitual or popular practices. However, the snippet is more about master teaching and professional development than a document dedicated to why teachers should always review instruction.","learned":"Teachers should review instruction because teaching excellence requires ongoing development and because habits or superficially appealing practices may not improve classroom learning."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q560","reason":"The reflective-practitioner query may surface more direct sections or dedicated sources about reflective teaching and continuous instructional improvement."}}}
