{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-224279","model_call":45,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ teachers \"formative assessment\" \"adjust instruction\" (+ improve learning students))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ teachers \"formative assessment\" \"adjust instruction\" (+ improve learning students))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1618177656#0_3400137717","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q572","rank":1,"snippet":"formative assessments to gauge student understanding has become more important than ever during COVID-19, when many teachers and students are working in blended learning environments for the first time. But it can be tough to switch from being test-oriented to learning-oriented. Focusing on learning-oriented techniques like formative assessment may be a bit more time consuming and require some extra strategy, but the results are well worth the effort. That’s because these types of diagnostic check-ins are essential to guiding lesson planning and adjusting instruction to support <cover>students’ growth. The Strengths of Formative Assessment When teachers constantly monitor student growth and adjust instruction </cover>to ensure continuous improvement, they find it easier and more predictable to progress towards meeting the standards on summative assessments. By understanding exactly what their students know before and during instruction, educators have much more power to improve student mastery of the subject matter than if they find out after a lesson or unit is complete. The strength of formative assessment lies in the critical information it provides about student comprehension throughout the learning process and the opportunity it gives educators to provide students with timely and action-oriented feedback and to "},"time":241},"model_calls":45,"document_judgments":33,"relevance_3":20,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
